NAME
    md - render a Markdown file on the console

SYNOPSIS
    md [-p] file.md
    md

DESCRIPTION
    Renders a Markdown file as styled, paged console text, so the
    documents in /DOCS read well at the machine itself. A name that is
    not found as given (and does not start with /) is looked for in
    /DOCS, so md OS.MD reads /DOCS/OS.MD. With no argument md prints
    its usage and lists the documents in /DOCS. "md: not found" when
    neither exists. md is /BIN/MD.

    The subset, one classification per source line:
        # .. ######   headings, bold; levels 1 and 2 also underlined
        - * +  1.     list items, with a hanging indent
        >             quotes, behind a "  | " gutter
        ```           code fences: verbatim, indented, dim
        | a | b |     tables: columns measured and aligned, the ---
                      row drawn as dashes
        --- *** ___   a rule across the screen
    Everything else is prose, word-wrapped at 78 columns; consecutive
    lines (and a list item's indented continuation lines) join into
    one paragraph. Inline **bold** and `code` become attributes, and
    [text](url) prints as text (url). Anything outside the subset
    passes through as prose, so rendering never fails.

    Styling is plain ANSI SGR escapes, which any terminal emulator
    shows. -p leaves every escape out and underlines headings with a
    row of = or - instead; the layout is otherwise the same.

    Output goes through the --More-- pager (see pager): after 23 lines
    a key, read without echo: space = the next page, Enter = one more
    line, q or Q (or Ctrl-D) = quit.

OPTIONS
    -p    plain: no escape sequences (dumb terminals, tests)

LIMITS
    Source lines are cut at 238 characters; a table holds 20 rows of
    178 characters and 10 columns.

EXAMPLES
    md                   the usage and the list of /DOCS
    md OS.MD             Y1/OS: the shell, the file API, memory
    md -p /DOCS/Y1CC.MD  the C compiler, plain
    md PORT.MD           the plan behind the /BIN commands

FILES
    /DOCS/OS.MD       os/README.md: Y1/OS itself
    /DOCS/Y1CC.MD     the y1cc C compiler
    /DOCS/OSPLAN.MD   the plan for the disk OS
    /DOCS/PORT.MD     the port of the P8X commands
    /DOCS/KERMIT.MD   the Mac side of kermit (C-Kermit settings)
    /DOCS/MDDEMO.MD   one example of everything md renders

NOTES
    On the instruction-level emulator a lower-case q is read as end of
    input, which quits as well.
    Changed from the P8X version: the shared pager (23 lines, was 22,
    with a plain prompt), the /DOCS fallback and list, and paragraphs
    that join the lines of a hard-wrapped source (the P8X rendered
    every source line as a paragraph of its own).
    Ported from P8X os/commands/md.c 2026-09-23.

